<p class="sst-lead">A decision is only as good as its stability. Sweep the effect size and watch the calibrated
test's <strong>rejection rate</strong> &mdash; and its ambiguity.</p>

<p>\[ \text{ambiguity} = 4\,\mathbb{P}_n(H=1)\big(1-\mathbb{P}_n(H=1)\big),\qquad H\in\{0,1\}. \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>At \(\delta=0\) the rejection rate sits at the test size \(\alpha\) (correctly calibrated). As the effect grows
  it climbs to 1. In between is the <strong>ambiguous band</strong> where \(4p(1-p)\to1\) and the verdict is a
  coin flip.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">A nuisance parameter &mdash; segmentation smoothing, regularization dimension \(r\) &mdash; slides
  you along \(\delta\). The "Goldilocks" failure is landing in that band: too little or too much smoothing and the
  decision stops being trustworthy. Honest reporting means showing where you sit on this curve.</p>
</div>
